INSERT INTO grammar_chapters (
  id, module_id, slug, title, position, estimated_min, difficulty,
  band_target, summary, content, is_published
)
VALUES (
  'a1000001-0001-4000-8000-000000000101',
  'a1000001-0001-4000-8000-000000000001',
  'ch1-subject-verb-agreement',
  'Subject-Verb Agreement & Sentence Foundations',
  1,
  30,
  2,
  'Band 6 accuracy',
  'Master the core agreement rule and the eight cases that decide your mark -- the highest-value, lowest-effort accuracy gain in the whole exam.',
  $content${"version":1,"blocks":[{"id":"b01","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"1.1 First, the building blocks"},{"id":"b02","type":"paragraph","text":"Every basic English sentence has two essential parts and often a third: the subject (who or what the sentence is about), the verb (the action or state), and sometimes an object (what receives the action)."},{"id":"b03","type":"paragraph","text":"The normal English order is Subject + Verb + Object (S + V + O). Example: Students (S) use (V) the internet (O)."},{"id":"b04","type":"callout","variant":"regional_tip","title":"REGIONAL TIP (Bangla speakers)","text":"Bangla is a Subject-Object-Verb language. The Bangla sentence for 'I eat rice' puts the verb at the end. English puts the verb second, right after the subject: \"I eat rice.\" When an English sentence you write feels wrong, the first thing to check is whether the verb has drifted to the end out of Bangla habit."},{"id":"b05","type":"paragraph","text":"\"Agreement\" simply means matching. Subject-verb agreement is the rule that the verb must match its subject in number. A singular subject takes one form of the verb; a plural subject takes another."},{"id":"b06","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"1.2 The core rule"},{"id":"b07","type":"paragraph","text":"In the present simple tense, the verb changes depending on whether the subject is singular or plural."},{"id":"b08","type":"table","headers":["Subject","Verb form","Example"],"rows":[["Singular (he, she, it, one thing)","verb + s / es","The student works hard."],["Plural (they, more than one)","base verb, no -s","The students work hard."],["I / You / We / They","base verb, no -s","They work hard."]]},{"id":"b09","type":"callout","variant":"warning","title":"The reversed -s trap","text":"On a noun, -s means plural: one student, many students. On a verb, -s means singular: he works, they work. So a correct sentence often has an -s on either the noun or the verb, but not both: \"The student works\" / \"The students work\"."},{"id":"b10","type":"table","headers":["Subject","be (present)","be (past)","have (present)"],"rows":[["Singular (he/she/it, one thing)","is","was","has"],["Plural / I / you / we / they","are","were","have"]]},{"id":"b11","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"1.3 The cases that actually decide your mark"},{"id":"b12","type":"paragraph","text":"The core rule is easy. Marks are lost on the cases below, where the true subject is hidden, disguised, or further away from the verb than a nearer noun. Master these eight and you remove almost every agreement error from your writing."},{"id":"b13","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Case 1 - Words between the subject and the verb"},{"id":"b14","type":"paragraph","text":"The verb agrees with the true subject, never with the nearest noun. Technique: find the subject, then mentally delete every word between it and the verb."},{"id":"b15","type":"example_pair","incorrect":"The list of items are on the table.","correct":"The list of items is on the table.","why":"The subject is \"list\" (singular), not \"items\"."},{"id":"b16","type":"example_pair","incorrect":"The quality of the answers were poor.","correct":"The quality of the answers was poor.","why":"Subject is \"quality\", not \"answers\"."},{"id":"b17","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Case 2 - \"The number of\" versus \"a number of\""},{"id":"b18","type":"paragraph","text":"\"The number of + plural noun\" takes a singular verb (the subject is number). \"A number of + plural noun\" takes a plural verb (it means several). This pair appears constantly in Writing Task 1."},{"id":"b19","type":"example_pair","incorrect":"The number of tourists have increased since 2010.","correct":"The number of tourists has increased since 2010.","why":"\"The number of\" is singular."},{"id":"b20","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Case 3 - Collective nouns"},{"id":"b21","type":"paragraph","text":"Words such as government, team, committee, family, company, public, staff name a group. In IELTS academic writing, treat each as a single unit and use a singular verb."},{"id":"b22","type":"example_pair","incorrect":"The government are planning three new policies.","correct":"The government is planning three new policies.","why":"Collective noun as one unit -> singular in academic style."},{"id":"b23","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Case 4 - Indefinite pronouns are singular"},{"id":"b24","type":"paragraph","text":"Each, every, everyone, everybody, someone, anyone, nobody, either, neither, one are all singular, however plural the words around them look."},{"id":"b25","type":"example_pair","incorrect":"Each of the students have a laptop.","correct":"The each of the students has a laptop.","why":"\"Each of\" is always singular."},{"id":"b26","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Case 5 - Neither...nor / either...or / not only...but also"},{"id":"b27","type":"paragraph","text":"With these paired connectors, the verb agrees with the nearer subject -- the one closest to the verb."},{"id":"b28","type":"example_pair","incorrect":"Neither the manager nor the employees was informed.","correct":"Neither the manager nor the employees were informed.","why":"Nearer subject is \"employees\" (plural)."},{"id":"b29","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Case 6 - Compound subjects joined by \"and\""},{"id":"b30","type":"paragraph","text":"Two subjects joined by and are almost always plural. Exception (rare in IELTS): when the two words describe a single combined thing -- \"Bread and butter is a common breakfast.\""},{"id":"b31","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Case 7 - \"There is\" and \"there are\""},{"id":"b32","type":"paragraph","text":"After there, the verb agrees with the real subject, which comes after the verb: There is a serious problem. / There are several factors."},{"id":"b33","type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Case 8 - Uncountable nouns, amounts, and inverted structures"},{"id":"b34","type":"paragraph","text":"Uncountable nouns are treated as singular and never take a plural -s. High-frequency IELTS set: information, advice, knowledge, research, equipment, furniture, accommodation, traffic, progress, news, money, water, work."},{"id":"b35","type":"example_pair","incorrect":"The informations are correct and he gave many advices.","correct":"The information is correct and he gave a lot of advice.","why":"Information and advice are uncountable."},{"id":"b36","type":"paragraph","text":"Amounts of time, money, and distance are treated as a single quantity: Ten years is a long time to wait. Five thousand taka is not enough."},{"id":"b37","type":"l1_error_fixer","title":"Why Bangla speakers make agreement mistakes","body":"Almost every subject-verb agreement error traces back to one fact: the Bangla verb does not change its ending to match a singular or plural subject the way the English verb does. Because your first language never trained this switch, your ear does not warn you when it is missing. The fix is a deliberate proofreading pass: after writing, go through every present-tense verb, find its true subject, and check the match on purpose.","error_types":[{"label":"Dropped third-person -s","incorrect":"He go to the market every day. / The population increase rapidly.","correct":"He goes to the market every day. / The population increases rapidly."},{"label":"Dropped or wrong is / are","incorrect":"Many people is using the internet. / The graph show that pollution have increased.","correct":"Many people are using the internet. / The graph shows that pollution has increased."}]},{"id":"b38","type":"ielts_impact","skills":["writing","speaking"],"title":"Why this one rule caps your band","body":"Subject-verb agreement is the error examiners notice first, because it appears in the very first line of almost every answer and it repeats. A script with recurring agreement errors cannot reach Band 7 in Grammatical Range and Accuracy, however good the ideas are. The damage is heaviest in Writing Task 1, which lives on subjects like the number of, the proportion of, the figure for -- every one of which is singular.","examples":{"task1":"The number of visitors has risen sharply, whereas the proportion of local tourists has fallen.","task2":"Although the government provides free healthcare, demand continues to outstrip supply.","speaking":"My family lives in a small town, and each of my brothers has a different job."}},{"id":"b39","type":"exercise_ref","exercise_slug":"ex-1-1-identification","label":"Exercise 1.1 - Identification and Correction"},{"id":"b40","type":"exercise_ref","exercise_slug":"ex-1-2-essay-edit","label":"Exercise 1.2 - IELTS Essay Editing"}]}$content$::jsonb,
  true
)
